---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { fetchSources } from '../lib/api';

// The table is served from the live source registry, so this page states what is actually
// running rather than what was true when the copy was written.
const sources = await fetchSources();
const REPO_URL = 'https://github.com/jagguvarma15/researchscout';
---

<Base title="About">
  <div class="about">
    <header class="hero">
      <h1 class="page-title">About ResearchScout</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A radar for AI and machine learning research: it watches new papers and the signals
        gathering around them, then ranks what is worth reading now. It is a personal,
        non-commercial project: these pages are hosted, and the index behind them runs on the
        operator's own machine.
      </p>
      <nav class="jump" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#data">Data sources</a>
        <a href="#licensing">Copyright</a>
        <a href="#apis">API use</a>
        <a href="#local">What stays local</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>How a paper reaches you</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Ingest.</strong> New submissions arrive from arXiv with their titles, abstracts,
          authors and categories.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Signals.</strong> Citation counts, Hugging Face upvotes, Hacker News points and
          Bluesky engagement attach to papers already stored.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Score and cluster.</strong> Those series become a momentum score - level,
          velocity and acceleration - and nearby papers group into emerging topics.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Answer.</strong> Ask Scout a question and it retrieves matching papers and cites
          each one, so every claim points back at a source you can open.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="data">
      <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Every source below is a connector that can be switched on or off in configuration. This
        table is read from the running registry, so it reflects what is enabled right now.
      </p>

      {
        sources === null && (
          <div class="card empty">
            <Icon name="lucide:cloud-off" aria-hidden="true" />
            <p>The source list is unavailable while the API is unreachable.</p>
          </div>
        )
      }

      {
        sources !== null && (
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="sources">
              {/* Fixed widths: the two prose columns hold full sentences, so letting the
                  browser size columns from content lets one long "provides" string starve
                  the rest. These proportions keep both prose columns equal. */}
              <colgroup>
                <col class="col-source" />
                <col class="col-status" />
                <col class="col-takes" />
                <col class="col-terms" />
              </colgroup>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Source</th>
                  <th scope="col">Status</th>
                  <th scope="col">What this site takes</th>
                  <th scope="col">Terms it arrives under</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {sources.map((source) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">
                      {source.homepage ? (
                        <a href={source.homepage} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
                          {source.display_name ?? source.name}
                        </a>
                      ) : (
                        (source.display_name ?? source.name)
                      )}
                      <span class="kind">{source.kind}</span>
                    </th>
                    <td>
                      <span class={`badge ${source.enabled ? 'on' : ''}`}>
                        {source.enabled ? 'on' : 'off'}
                      </span>
                    </td>
                    <td>{source.provides ?? 'Not documented'}</td>
                    <td>
                      {source.data_license ?? 'Not documented'}
                      {source.terms_url && (
                        <a
                          class="terms"
                          href={source.terms_url}
                          rel="noopener noreferrer"
                          target="_blank"
                        >
                          Read the terms
                        </a>
                      )}
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        )
      }
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="licensing">
      <h2>Copyright and licensing</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Paper metadata is free to reuse; papers are not.</strong> arXiv releases its
        metadata - titles, abstracts, authors, identifiers - under the <a
          href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank">CC0 1.0 public domain dedication</a
        >, which is what makes this feed possible. The papers themselves stay under their authors'
        copyright, on whichever license each author chose at submission: the arXiv non-exclusive
        license, CC BY, CC BY-SA, CC BY-NC-SA, CC BY-NC-ND or CC0.
      </p>
      <p>
        So this site does not redistribute papers. Every card links back to the original, and when
        you open the reader your browser fetches the PDF from arXiv directly - nothing is rehosted
        here. Article text is fetched only for papers you save or open, kept in the local database
        to make search work, and never returned by the API; answers quote at most a few hundred
        characters alongside a link to the paper.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Data from Semantic Scholar</strong>, whose API license requires this credit.
        Semantic Scholar data arrives under the licenses accompanying it, such as CC BY-NC or
        ODC-BY. Citation counts from OpenAlex, when that connector is enabled, are CC0.
      </p>
      <p>
        Posts on Hugging Face, Hacker News and Bluesky belong to the people who wrote them. This
        site stores only aggregates - upvote counts, story points and comment counts, engagement
        and reply counts, and the public Hacker News story ids - never post text and never author
        handles.
      </p>
      <p>
        The ResearchScout software is open source under the MIT license, Copyright (c) 2026
        Jagadesh Varma. Its <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"
          >source is on GitHub</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        ResearchScout is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or supported by arXiv, Semantic
        Scholar, Hugging Face, Hacker News, Bluesky, GitHub, OpenAlex or OpenReview.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="apis">
      <h2>How these APIs are used</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>
          <strong>One request at a time, three seconds apart.</strong> arXiv asks for no more than
          one request every three seconds on a single connection, and that floor is held across
          every arXiv call this app makes - paging, a second category, or a health check.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Requests identify themselves.</strong> Every outbound call sends a User-Agent
          naming this app and linking to its source, so an upstream can reach a person rather than
          block an address.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Incremental, never bulk.</strong> Fetches resume from a cursor over a recent
          window instead of harvesting a corpus, and article text is limited to papers you have
          actually saved or opened, in capped batches.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Credentials raise limits, they do not bypass them.</strong> An optional Semantic
          Scholar key, GitHub token or OpenAlex polite-pool address widens a quota on the
          provider's terms. Connectors that need one ship switched off.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing is republished.</strong> There is no bulk export, no public copy of
          what the sources provided, and no third party receiving any of it. What this site
          offers is a way to find papers and read them where they live.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="local">
      <h2>What stays on this machine</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>
          No analytics, no trackers, no third-party scripts, and no fonts or stylesheets loaded
          from a CDN - everything the page needs ships with it.
        </li>
        <li>
          The papers, the scores and everything an account holds live in a database on the
          operator's own machine, not in a cloud service. The pages you are reading are
          rendered by a host that reaches that machine over a private tunnel.
        </li>
        <li>
          Reading signals - what you open, save, dwell on or dismiss - are written to the local
          database and used only to personalize your feed.
        </li>
        <li>
          Answers are generated by a language model running on that same machine, so questions
          are not sent to a model provider. Point the configured model at a hosted service and
          they would go there instead.
        </li>
        <li>
          Two things do leave: your browser fetches PDFs from arXiv when you open the reader, and
          a web search in Scout sends that query to arXiv and Semantic Scholar to find papers this
          library does not have yet.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="after-list">
        The <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a> says the same thing in full, including what an
        account stores and how to delete it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Scout can be wrong</h2>
      <p>
        Summaries, topic labels and answers are machine-generated from stored abstracts and short
        excerpts. They can misread a paper or miss its caveats, and a momentum score measures
        attention rather than quality. Treat all of it as a way to find papers, then read the
        paper.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Something misattributed, or a source's terms read wrong here? Open an issue on <a
          href={REPO_URL}
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank">the repository</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* One centered column: the page is prose, so it reads as a document rather than a
     left-hugging column against empty space. Headings and the intro center with it;
     paragraph text stays left-aligned inside, which is what keeps long lines readable. */
  .about {
    max-width: 58rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .hero {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }
  .hero .page-title {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .lede {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  /* Inline text links rather than chips: hairline separators are drawn in CSS so no
     punctuation characters sit between them. */
  .jump {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.5rem 0.9rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
  .jump a {
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .jump a + a {
    padding-left: 0.9rem;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .jump a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .block {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
    padding-top: var(--space-5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .block h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .block p {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 auto 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .block p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .note {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .after-list {
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }
  .steps,
  .facts {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .steps li,
  .facts li {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .steps li:last-child,
  .facts li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-top: 1.2rem;
  }
  /* Centered on both axes, to sit with the centered headings and prose above it rather
     than hugging the left edge on its own. `fixed` is what makes the colgroup widths
     bind; without it the browser still sizes columns from their content. */
  .sources {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 46rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    table-layout: fixed;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .col-source {
    width: 22%;
  }
  .col-status {
    width: 12%;
  }
  .col-takes,
  .col-terms {
    width: 33%;
  }
  /* Padding on both sides now: the old rule zeroed the left side to sit flush against a
     left-aligned column, which is what left the columns crowded together. */
  .sources th,
  .sources td {
    padding: 0.8rem 1.25rem;
    vertical-align: middle;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .sources thead th {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
  /* Names wrap rather than hold one line: under a fixed column "Hacker News (Algolia API)"
     is wider than its share and would spill into the next cell. */
  .sources tbody th {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .sources tbody tr:last-child th,
  .sources tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: none;
  }
  .kind {
    display: block;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  .terms {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
  }
  .badge.on {
    background: var(--accent-soft);
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .sources {
      min-width: 34rem;
    }
    .sources th,
    .sources td {
      padding: 0.7rem 0.75rem;
    }
  }
</style>
